# Copy to .env.local (gitignored) and fill in your own values.
#
# WHERE THESE COME FROM, and it is no longer this file alone.
# Every VITE_* variable either web app reads is declared in
# scripts/client-secrets.mjs, and release step 0c fills it from Google Secret
# Manager before anything is built. This file is the LOCAL fallback: it is what
# your own `npm run dev` and `npm run build` use when nothing else supplies a
# value. Precedence, which is Vite's own:
#
#   1. process.env                     an explicit override; how CI passes a
#                                      repo secret into a preview build
#   2. .env.production.local           the release, from Secret Manager.
#                                      Generated, removed when it finishes, and
#                                      read only by a production build.
#   3. .env.local, .env                this file, filled in by you
#
# `node scripts/client-secrets.mjs --list` prints every variable, which Secret
# Manager secret backs it, and whether a release refuses without it.

# O-3 App Check (docs/O3_APP_CHECK_RULING_2026-07-13.md): local dev needs a
# debug token since localhost isn't an allowed domain on the App Check
# reCAPTCHA Enterprise key. Leave unset to auto-generate one on first run —
# the SDK prints it to the browser console; register that value in the
# console instead.
#
# THE ONE GENUINELY SENSITIVE NAME IN THIS FILE. Everything else here is a
# public client identifier that ships in the bundle. This is not: it BYPASSES
# App Check attestation for whoever holds it. It is per-developer, registered in
# the Firebase console (App Check > your web app > Manage debug tokens), and it
# must never be committed, stored in Secret Manager, or set in CI. One shared
# bypass token is a bypass token for everyone. Production builds never carry it:
# `import.meta.env.DEV` is statically false there, so the branch that reads it
# is tree-shaken out.
VITE_APPCHECK_DEBUG_TOKEN=

# Sentry crash reporting for the Kinfolk portal at kinfolk.tribetails.com.
# Secret Manager: PORTAL_WEB_SENTRY_DSN. A release WARNS without it, and today
# every release warns: this has never been set, here or in the store. Web Sentry
# is off for both web apps and that is a known, accepted state.
#
# The DSN belongs to the `mytribe-web` project in the `tribetails` Sentry org
# (Settings > Client Keys / DSN), a DIFFERENT project from the admin's, so a
# different DSN. That is why each app declares its own secret instead of the two
# sharing one name.
#
# Public by design: it permits write-only event ingestion, and what bounds abuse
# is Sentry-side rate limits and inbound filters. Leave it empty locally and
# reporting is simply off (the app logs "Sentry disabled: no VITE_SENTRY_DSN"
# and runs normally).
VITE_SENTRY_DSN=

# Tags events with a release so Sentry can group a regression against the deploy
# that introduced it. DERIVED, not stored: a release sets this to the commit it
# is shipping. Set it locally only if you want your own dev events tagged.
VITE_SENTRY_RELEASE=

# Mapbox public token for the KinCare route map (#520). It draws the visit
# route over real streets instead of a bare line on an empty background.
# Secret Manager: PORTAL_WEB_MAPBOX_PUBLIC_TOKEN. A release REFUSES without it.
#
# Use the URL-restricted `web-maps-public` token (scopes styles:tiles,
# styles:read, fonts:read; referrers kinfolk.tribetails.com and
# auntie.tribetails.com), NOT the unrestricted mobile one the Android apps use.
#
# A `pk.*` token is a public client credential by design: read-only, and what
# bounds the value of an extracted copy is the referrer restriction plus
# rotation, not secrecy.
#
# Empty locally costs you nothing: RouteMap draws the SVG polyline it always
# has, with no error and no blank frame. Empty in a RELEASE is the reason this
# one refuses: the site would look entirely healthy while quietly handing every
# kinfolk the plainer map, and nobody would think to check.
VITE_MAPBOX_PUBLIC_TOKEN=
